# Web / JavaScript ESM

Source package `appside`, exports `appside/web`; repository import shown below. Node tests executed; browser QA status is in CODEX-016 evidence.

```js
import {Appside} from './sdks/web/index.js';
const appside = new Appside({baseURL: 'https://your-studio-host'});
appside.configure('orbit', {environment: 'test', returnURL: 'https://game.example/app'});
appside.onPurchaseCompleted = result => refreshWalletFromBackend();
await appside.openStore(await freshTokenFromYourBackend());
await appside.purchase('voyager', await freshTokenFromYourBackend());
const result = appside.handle(location.href);
```

`openStore`/`purchase` return Promise<launch URL>. Default opener reserves a new browser tab before the request; call from a user gesture. `openURL` can be injected for an embedded host (Orbit Quest uses this). `fetch`, `storage` are injectable. sessionStorage preserves pending state across same-tab reloads for one hour. Receive the return on the configured host/tab or forward it to that SDK instance; the SDK does not install a cross-window message listener.

Run `npm test --prefix sdks/web`.

All purchases are TEST only. `PurchaseResult` (`status`, `orderId`, `state`, `verified=false`) is a UI hint; grant items only on your backend using verified webhooks or authenticated `getOrder`. Callback names: `onPurchaseCompleted`, `onPurchaseCanceled`, `onPurchaseFailed`, `onStoreClosed`. A successful launch is not a completed purchase.

Register the exact `returnURL` in Studio → Integration, configure your app's link handler, and pass incoming URLs to `handle`. Each launch needs a fresh backend-issued player token. The SDK correlates returns with pending launch state and rejects replay. Native pending state is in memory: after process death, reopen with a fresh token.

